\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Trial-level model comparison, response time, and the predicted resolution limit.} \textbf  {a}, Response-likelihood $\Delta $AIC over the ${\sim }53{,}000$ individual responses, per exposure duration and summed (Total), each cell relative to the best of its column (darker is better; shading scaled within each panel). Each well family (frequency-based width, Eq.~\eqref  {eq:width}, free $\beta $; or power-law width $\sigma _n \propto n^{p}$) appears with its clean-up dynamics and as a matched static readout of the same wells (no extra parameters): clean-up wins at the briefest exposures, the static readouts catch up at 320--640~ms, and in total the winning frequency-well attractor leads its static twin by $\approx 1260$, with the Weber/ANS code and the normative log-prior proxy far behind. \textbf  {b}, Response-time-channel $\Delta $AIC on the winning landscape, same layout. The link combining the attractor's convergence time and the residual readout entropy is best at every exposure; convergence time alone stays close, while the static entropy proxy falls behind at 160--640~ms. \textbf  {c}, The fitted width law $\sigma _n$ per exposure duration. Wells are resolvable ($\sigma _n < 1$) at small $n$ and merge ($\sigma _n \ge 1$) beyond the critical numerosity $n_{\mathrm  {crit}}$ (dots), which grows from ${\approx }4.6$ at 40~ms to ${\approx }10$ at 640~ms: more exposure sharpens the wells and extends the exact range.}}{4}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:comparison}{{2}{4}{\textbf {Trial-level model comparison, response time, and the predicted resolution limit.} \textbf {a}, Response-likelihood $\Delta $AIC over the ${\sim }53{,}000$ individual responses, per exposure duration and summed (Total), each cell relative to the best of its column (darker is better; shading scaled within each panel). Each well family (frequency-based width, Eq.~\eqref {eq:width}, free $\beta $; or power-law width $\sigma _n \propto n^{p}$) appears with its clean-up dynamics and as a matched static readout of the same wells (no extra parameters): clean-up wins at the briefest exposures, the static readouts catch up at 320--640~ms, and in total the winning frequency-well attractor leads its static twin by $\approx 1260$, with the Weber/ANS code and the normative log-prior proxy far behind. \textbf {b}, Response-time-channel $\Delta $AIC on the winning landscape, same layout. The link combining the attractor's convergence time and the residual readout entropy is best at every exposure; convergence time alone stays close, while the static entropy proxy falls behind at 160--640~ms. \textbf {c}, The fitted width law $\sigma _n$ per exposure duration. Wells are resolvable ($\sigma _n < 1$) at small $n$ and merge ($\sigma _n \ge 1$) beyond the critical numerosity $n_{\mathrm {crit}}$ (dots), which grows from ${\approx }4.6$ at 40~ms to ${\approx }10$ at 640~ms: more exposure sharpens the wells and extends the exact range}{figure.caption.13}{}}
